s to be the case that classic films  are being chosen not because they'd  benefit from the live-action format but  because they're popular as I said  different stories ask for different  things from its visuals and if the story  that's being told is focusing on aspects  that are not enhanced by the visuals you  get something like this now this is not  to suggest that live-action remakes or  reimagining x' are a bad idea in fact I  think with the right properties they are  a great idea and have a chance at being  superior to the originals if chosen for  the right reasons and done correctly as  I said the four major components for any  story  our unique combination of plot setting  theme and character if an animated  feature deals with a gritty realistic  world of human characters it stands to  reason a live-action adaptation could  add to these areas of the film in ways  animation could not however there is a  massive problem a stigma that animation  hasn't been able to shake in decades at  this moment in time the mainstream  belief is that live-action is an  objective improvement higher-quality and  for all ages all the while the unique  advantages animation provides us with  our being ignored or forgotten simply  cast aside as something for children  let's talk about the Lion King the  staggering level of detail and  perfection that these photorealistic  animals March chat and sing across the  screen with is nothing short of  incredible so much so you'd be forgiven  for forgetting that these are actually  CGI models generated on a computer it's  abundantly clear that an absurd amount  of time effort and skill went into  making this animated world as true to  the real world  as possible which makes what I'm about  to say all the more difficult the  visuals don't communicate effectively  and I think the creator of Toy Story  said it best one of my mentors whose  work really really always inspired me  was Chuck Jones who was a great Warner  Brothers cartoon director he always said  John great animation you should turn the  sound off and still be able to tell  what's going on you can pick any scene  at random from the original Lion King  film and without context you can  understand what the characters are  feeling and even what their motivations  are for instance if you take images from  this scene in the original film and try  to decipher what the emotions and  motivations behind the characters were  you'd find it's remarkably easy this  isn't by accident the purpose of the  visuals behind any story is to enhance  the communication of the narrative  because of this it's possible to watch  the original Lion King without sound and  still understand what's going on however  if that same approach was taken with the  live-action film you'll find that you  receive a tiny fraction of the  information in comparison in saying that  I realize it might prompt some of you to  say what doesn't matter if they're  communicating visually they're talking I  get the information that way in saying  this you have made an argument for radio  if the  don't matter why not whip some vaguely  similar footage from a bunch of wildlife  documentaries and call it a day I'll  tell you why because more information is  communicated through visuals than  through dialogue and the best example of  that is this where the staggering up a  hill the inclusion of a knowing smile  and the gentle glide of a balloon  towards a hospital bed can take you  through a whirlwind of emotions unlike  anything dialog can grant you in that  time if you don't understand what visual  storytelling is I implore you to watch  Disney Pixar's on within the first 15  minutes you will have an intimate  understanding of what it is and why it's  important now don't get me wrong  live-action films can and have famously  told their stories without words but as  we continue to push the limits of CGI  into the realm of photo realism we're  starting to lose sight of what the  visuals of a film are actually therefore  to communicate emotion to communicate  motive to tell a story and I think no  other live-action remake better embodies  that mistake than The Lion King in order  to overcompensate for its lack of  expression the character models in the  Lion King are forced to express  themselves through body language funnily  enough for the exact same reasons the  Power Rangers overact when they're in  the ranger suits because their faces  cannot be seen  additionally in scenes that would  utilize an abundance of color to  emphasize the fantastical imagination  and naivety of a character's youth this  photorealistic aesthetic is forced to  downplay these moments in an effort to  make it more uniform with the rest of  the production the result is something  much less visually striking and  interesting and doesn't serve the  character and setting as effectively as  the animated Lion King did the more I  analyze this film's use of CGI for  realism the more it makes me realize  that it's less an effective storytelling  device and more so an impressive tech  demonstration something that will have  people say man that looks gorgeous and  it is gorgeous and impressive and all  the other things people have been saying  online about it but it's not a good  storytelling device exhausting  everything it has to offer in the first  acts admittedly gorgeous opening  sequence however there is one place  where traditional 2d animation is still  King and unlike anywhere else is not  stigmatized as simply children's  entertainment  Japan unlike the United States in  Hollywood the Japanese anime industry  has wall-to-wall variation in its  animated products and at this point I  think it's safe to say that there's  nothing they won't animate unlike the  West where they relegate anime to its  own genre in the East anime covers  anything and everything from choose  entertainment to tragic war stories to  epic romances panning lifetimes and  action adventure thriller Ides anime as  